//? Title: Token Selection: Temperature, Top-k, Nucleus and Beam Search
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The exact upstream preparation protocol and the complete editable native C++ experiment preserve original source rows, seeds, fitting budgets and validation. Canonical components expose mathematical graphs; every objective, optimizer step, fitting/generation loop and deployment comparison remains visible.
)
\toc

> Replay the upstream sampling protocol

The source protocol generates 32 tokens for each decoding method from
the prompt `First Citizen:`. Greedy decoding retains one token.
Temperature .65 sharpens the softmax, top-k retains five tokens, and
nucleus sampling retains enough tokens to reach 80% of the mass. All
four reuse the same random stream. The source accumulates probabilities
in descending score order when drawing a token. The matched-width beam
uses newline as EOS and generates up to 12 tokens. This report replays
that protocol using its native trained checkpoint.

> Follow the actual data and model

Logits -> deterministic filtering -> caller uniform / beam loop.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/token_sampling_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -lpthread -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
